Protocol Target Lab third-party source notes — phase 1

The project's original Rust manager, Web application, HTTP worker, test client,
and integration scripts are distributed under the accompanying MIT LICENSE.
Third-party components retain their original licenses. Full license and copyright
texts for the locked Linux Rust dependency closure and bundled Web components
are in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt. The version/source/file index is
third-party/licenses.json (third-party-licenses.json in the installed docs).
The deb installs both files under /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab. Keep LICENSE,
this notice, the full license bundle and its index with standalone binaries.

The bundle includes nested upstream notices and the complete Vite notice file.
The include_dir crates omit their license text from their published archives;
third-party/upstream/include_dir preserves the license from the exact upstream
commit recorded in those archives. Generation and verification require only
prepared Cargo/npm caches; scripts/collect-third-party-licenses.py never downloads.

Rust dependencies:
  Cargo.toml lists direct dependencies. Cargo.lock records exact resolved package
  versions and registry checksums. Crate license declarations and upstream source
  metadata are available through `cargo metadata --locked --format-version 1`.
  Dependency source archives and LICENSE/NOTICE files must accompany any future
  complete offline source distribution; a Cargo lockfile alone is not that bundle.

Web dependencies:
  frontend/package.json lists direct dependencies. frontend/package-lock.json
  records package versions, source locations, integrity values and supplied license
  metadata. Preserve upstream license files when distributing dependency materials.

HTTP runtime image:
  Python 3.12, Python Software Foundation License, https://www.python.org/psf/license/
  Official Python image build sources: https://github.com/docker-library/python
  Debian base packages retain their package licenses in /usr/share/doc inside the
  image. images/http/base-image.lock identifies the selected public base digest.
  The worker uses only the Python standard library; no pip packages are installed.

Docker Engine and CLI:
  The manager invokes the system Docker CLI with structured arguments. The engine
  and its CLI are provided by the Ubuntu docker.io system package, not copied
  into this source repository or the manager deb. Upstream: https://github.com/moby/moby and
  https://github.com/docker/cli. Docker installation/dependency preparation is an
  administrator or offline-bundle preparation step, never a package-maintainer
  script download.

This first-stage notice describes implemented HTTP components. Other protocol
libraries and a complete offline distribution are not included or claimed here.
